    {
      "id": "s17",
      "type": "SAD_PATHS",
      "transition": "zoom",
      "background": "zoneB",
      "narration": "Same app, three times over. Nothing changed except what the server said back. First, the happy path. Everybody tests that one. Second, an empty list. Does the copy read properly with nothing to show? Third, a five hundred. Friendly error, or white screen? One line each.",
      "data": {
        "sadPaths": {
          "screenTitle": "Your products",
          "rows": [
            "Blue mug",
            "Red mug",
            "Green mug"
          ],
          "emptyText": "No products yet",
          "errorText": "Something went wrong",
          "caption": "one line each. every single run.",
          "color": "green",
          "atWord": 2,
          "states": [
            {
              "label": "the happy path",
              "title": "ok",
              "text": "json=[...three products...]",
              "sub": "everyone tests this",
              "atWord": 10.167
            },
            {
              "label": "an empty list",
              "title": "empty",
              "text": "route.fulfill(json=[])",
              "sub": "does the copy read ok?",
              "atWord": 19.25
            },
            {
              "label": "a 500 error",
              "title": "error",
              "text": "route.fulfill(status=500)",
              "sub": "or a white screen?",
              "atWord": 29.417
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 582,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-14-network_long_s17.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s18",
      "type": "CODE_RUN",
      "transition": "push",
      "background": "zoneA",
      "narration": "Simulating a server on fire takes one argument. Status five hundred, a bit of error json, and you're done. Then you load the page and check the thing you actually care about. Did anybody build a decent error screen? Is there a retry button, or does the user just sit there staring at nothing?",
      "data": {
        "codeRun": {
          "filename": "sad.py",
          "language": "py",
          "resultLabel": "what the app does",
          "color": "red",
          "atWord": 2.583,
          "caption": "the server is on fire, on demand",
          "lines": [
            {
              "text": "page.route(\"**/api/products\",",
              "detail": "catch the products call",
              "sub": "the desk is staffed",
              "label": "route",
              "atWord": 8.167
            },
            {
              "text": "    lambda route: route.fulfill(",
              "detail": "and answer with a 500",
              "sub": "the server \"died\"",
              "label": "boom",
              "atWord": 16.083
            },
            {
              "text": "        status=500,"
            },
            {
              "text": "        json={\"error\": \"Server Error\"}))"
            },
            {
              "text": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "expect(page.get_by_text(",
              "detail": "now check the error screen",
              "sub": "friendly, or a blank page?",
              "label": "check",
              "atWord": 28.75
            },
            {
              "text": "    \"Something went wrong\"))"
            },
            {
              "text": "    .to_be_visible()"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 535,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-14-network_long_s18.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s19",
      "type": "RULE_TEST",
      "transition": "slide",
      "background": "zoneB",
      "narration": "And that, more or less, is the interview answer. Everyone tests the happy path. Mocking is what makes the sad ones cheap — errors, empty states, slow replies — all reliable, all on demand, whenever you want them. Whole classes of scenario that used to be filed under untestable turn into one-liners.",
      "data": {
        "ruleTest": {
          "kicker": "say this out loud",
          "rule": "everyone tests the happy path. mock the sad ones.",
          "okLabel": "one line",
          "noLabel": "impossible",
          "caption": "whole classes of untestable, solved",
          "color": "red",
          "atWord": 2.833,
          "cases": [
            {
              "text": "mock a 500 and check the copy",
              "title": "ok",
              "sub": "any time you like",
              "atWord": 20.917
            },
            {
              "text": "ask production to fail on cue",
              "title": "no",
              "sub": "good luck with that",
              "atWord": 28.167
            },
            {
              "text": "mock an empty list",
              "title": "ok",
              "sub": "the state nobody designs",
              "atWord": 32.667
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 586,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-14-network_long_s19.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s20",
      "type": "RECAP",
      "transition": "fade",
      "background": "zoneA",
      "narration": "Three lines to take away. Expect response waits for a real letter, instead of guessing. Route puts you behind the desk, where every request has to pass. And fulfill lets you write whatever reply you fancy — including the ones nobody can ask a real server for.",
      "data": {
        "heading": "The mail room, in three lines",
        "points": [
          {
            "text": "expect_response waits for real data",
            "atWord": 11.5
          },
          {
            "text": "route() intercepts before it leaves",
            "atWord": 19.583
          },
          {
            "text": "fulfill() writes the reply yourself",
            "atWord": 26.083
          }
        ]
      },
      "durationFrames": 477,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-14-network_long_s20.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
